## Why This Tool Is Worth Keeping Handy

- Native AI integration with OpenAI, Claude Code, Ollama, LM Studio, and other
  OpenAI-compatible endpoints
- Kubernetes-native log streaming and stdin/file workflows in the same tool
- Real-time charts and filters that make anomalies easier to spot before you
  ask AI for help
- Optional browser dashboard (`Dstl8.Lite`) launched from inside Gonzo with the
  `d` key

## Quick Start

Analyze a file:

```bash
gonzo -f application.log
```

Follow a file:

```bash
gonzo -f /var/log/app.log --follow
```

Read from stdin:

```bash
cat application.log | gonzo
```

Use Gonzo's native Kubernetes mode:

```bash
gonzo --k8s-enabled=true
```

## AI Provider Patterns

OpenAI:

```bash
export OPENAI_API_KEY="sk-..."
gonzo -f logs.json --ai-model="gpt-4"
```

Ollama:

```bash
export OPENAI_API_KEY="ollama"
export OPENAI_API_BASE="http://localhost:11434"
gonzo -f logs.json --follow
```

Claude Code:

```bash
gonzo --ai-provider=claude-code -f logs.json
gonzo --ai-provider=claude-code --ai-model=haiku -f /var/log/app.log --follow
```

Practical note:

- OpenAI/Ollama/LM Studio expose models through API discovery.
- Claude Code manages auth separately through the Claude CLI.
- Gonzo can auto-select a sensible default model when you omit `--ai-model`.

## Interactive AI Troubleshooting Flow

Gonzo is especially useful when you want an interactive, terminal-first AI log
companion instead of building custom prompt plumbing. Review the live stream,
open the relevant log details, and use Gonzo's AI analysis flow to ask for
pattern or anomaly explanations against the exact log context already in view.

